  "raw_text": "Step 1: Consider the double sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j+k)^3}\\).\n\nStep 2: Change the order of summation. The original sum can be rewritten by grouping terms based on the value of \\(n = j + k\\). For a fixed \\(n\\), \\(j\\) ranges from 1 to \\(n-1\\) and \\(k = n - j\\).\n\nStep 3: Rewrite the double sum as follows:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j+k)^3} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\frac{1}{n^3}.\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Simplify the inner sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \\frac{1}{n^3} = \\frac{n-1}{n^3}.\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Substitute this back into the outer sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{n-1}{n^3} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right).\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Split the sum into two separate sums:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3}.\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Recognize that \\(\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = p - 1\\) and \\(\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} = q - 1\\), where \\(p = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2}\\) and \\(q = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}\\).\n\nStep 8: Substitute these values into the expression:\n\\[\n(p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q.\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{p - q}<|im_end|>",
